<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Who Will Perform in the Fiestas Patrias Activities? – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 10px;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Talents Around Us</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Who Will Perform in the Fiestas Patrias Activities?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: productive: Present basic information about a hobby or interest. interactive: Exchange directions.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can present details of their performance to classmates (e.g., 'I will sing two songs in the Fiestas Patrias activities.' 'Liz is the Tambor Mayor. She enjoys leading the drummers in the parade, marking the rhythms, and keeping the group organized.'). Can ask for directions related to the talent show location (e.g., 'Where is the stage?'). Can provide basic information about their talent (e.g., 'I can play the drums in the parade.').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 55%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 10%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays pictures of different Fiestas Patrias activities (a parade with a band, a baton twirler, students dancing traditional dances, an honor roll student carrying the flag). The teacher asks students: "What do you see in the pictures?" "What talents do these students have?" "Will you perform in any Fiestas Patrias activities?" Students respond with simple phrases. The teacher reviews key vocabulary: <i>parade, flag, band, baton twirler, dance, sing, drums, accordion, flute</i>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models target structures: "I <b>will</b> play the drums in the parade." "I <b>can</b> sing a patriotic song." "<b>If</b> I practice my dance, I <b>will</b> perform beautifully." The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of minimal pairs in short vowels, such as /ʌ/ in 'fun' and 'drum', and /æ/ in 'band' and 'flag'. The teacher also demonstrates distinguishing rhyming words related to Panama's National Holidays, for example, 'drum' and 'hum' versus 'flag' and 'march'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding with Concept Checking Questions:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'I will march in the parade', am I doing it now or in the future?" (Expected answer: "In the future.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'I can play the accordion', does it mean I have the ability or I am nervous?" (Expected answer: "I have the ability.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "The Talents Around Us")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short dialogue between two friends, Leo and Sofia, discussing their plans for the Fiestas Patrias Talent Show. The dialogue integrates future simple, 'can', first conditional, and relevant vocabulary.
                <br><b>Dialogue: "Our Fiestas Patrias Talents"</b>
                <br><b>Leo:</b> "Hi Sofia! Are you excited for the Patriotic Celebrations?"
                <br><b>Sofia:</b> "Yes, Leo! I am very excited! I <b>will</b> dance two traditional dances. I <b>can</b> dance the Tamborito!"
                <br><b>Leo:</b> "That's amazing! I <b>can</b> play the flute. <b>If</b> I practice every day, I <b>will</b> perform beautifully!"
                <br><b>Sofia:</b> "Wow! Will you be in the parade with the band?"
                <br><b>Leo:</b> "Yes, I <b>will</b>! And after the parade, I will go to the talent show. Where is the stage for the dance performances?"
                <br><b>Sofia:</b> "The stage is near the big flag. Go straight, past the cafeteria, and turn right. You <b>will</b> see many colorful costumes!"
                <br><b>Leo:</b> "Great! I want to see your performance! Congratulations on being so talented!"
                <br><b>Sofia:</b> "Thanks! ¡Viva Panamá!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Share performance plans, express ability, express possibilities using conditionals, and exchange directions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What are Leo and Sofia talking about?" (Fiestas Patrias, talents, performances)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What talents do they have?" (Sofia can dance, Leo can play the flute.)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b>
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Sofia will sing two songs." (False)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Leo will play the flute in the band." (True)</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The stage for dance is next to the cafeteria." (True, after going straight and turning right.)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Speaking, focusing on future simple for plans, modal 'can' for ability, first conditional for possibilities, and vocabulary related to Fiestas Patrias talents and events (<i>talent, show, stage, performance, audience, costume, song, dance, art, practice, event, skill, hobby, drums, accordion, flute, performer, prepare, show, play, act, sing, dance, perform, march, excited, talented, ready</i>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Sentence Completion (5 min)</b>
                        <br>The teacher provides sentence starters or cloze sentences on the board or a worksheet, and students complete them orally or individually in pairs.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I ____ (play) the drums in the parade." (Expected: "I <b>will play</b>...")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I ____ (practice) a lot, I ____ (perform) beautifully." (Expected: "If I <b>practice</b> a lot, I <b>will perform</b>...")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"My friend ____ (can/sing) a patriotic song." (Expected: "My friend <b>can sing</b>...")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"The baton twirler ____ (can/twirl) her baton well." (Expected: "The baton twirler <b>can twirl</b>...")</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"This year, I ____ (be) an honor roll student for the parade." (Expected: "This year, I <b>will be</b>...")</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: Substitution Drill (5 min)</b>
                        <br>The teacher presents sentence frames on the board. Students work in pairs, taking turns to substitute words from a list to create new sentences orally.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Sentence Frame 1: "I will ____ the ____." (Options: <i>play / drums, sing / song, dance / traditional dance, march / parade, wave / flag</i>)
                                <br>Example: Student A: "I will play the drums." Student B: "I will sing a song."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Sentence Frame 2: "If I ____, I will ____." (Options: <i>practice / perform well, rehearse / be ready, try my best / win, dance well / impress the audience</i>)
                                <br>Example: Student A: "If I practice, I will perform well." Student B: "If I rehearse, I will be ready."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Sentence Frame 3: "I can ____." (Options: <i>play the accordion, sing, dance, draw, twirl the baton</i>)
                                <br>Example: Student A: "I can play the accordion." Student B: "I can sing."</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction: "Talent Q&A" (5 min)</b>
                        <br>Students work in pairs. They ask each other questions about their imagined talents and plans for Fiestas Patrias, using the target grammar. The teacher circulates, provides feedback on pronunciation (especially minimal pairs and rhyming words) and grammar, and encourages polite language for collaboration.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What will you do for Fiestas Patrias?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What talent do you have? What can you do?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If you practice [your talent], what will happen?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Where is the stage for [talent name]?"</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Paired Role-Play: "Fiestas Patrias Talent Show Interview."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in pairs. One student is an "Interviewer" for the school newspaper, and the other is a "Performer" for the upcoming Fiestas Patrias Talent Show.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The Interviewer asks about the Performer's talents, plans, and preparations for the show. The Performer shares details and can also ask for directions to different performance areas (e.g., "Where is the stage for the dance group?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students must use:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Future simple to describe plans (e.g., "I will sing two songs.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Modal 'can' to express ability (e.g., "I can play the accordion.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">First conditional for possibilities (e.g., "If I practice a lot, I will be ready.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Target vocabulary (e.g., <i>talent, performance, stage, audience, costume, rehearse, excited, amazing</i>).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Basic directions (e.g., "Go straight, turn left, it's next to the flag.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Sociolinguistic expressions where appropriate (e.g., "Congratulations!," "¡Viva Panamá!").</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After 3-4 minutes, students switch roles.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher encourages fluency and communicative success over perfect accuracy, making notes for common errors to address later.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and peer assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the "Fiestas Patrias Talent Show Interview" role-play in front of another pair or the teacher.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> Clear pronunciation of key vocabulary and minimal pairs (/ʌ/ and /æ/).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Grammar:</b> Accurate use of future simple, modal 'can', and first conditional.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary Usage:</b> Appropriate use of at least 5-7 target vocabulary words related to talents and Fiestas Patrias.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Fluency:</b> Able to communicate ideas and plans clearly and with reasonable flow.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Interaction:</b> Collaborates respectfully with peers, using polite language and responding appropriately to questions.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick self-reflection:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can talk about my talents using 'I can...'." (Yes / No / A little)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can share my plans for Fiestas Patrias using 'I will...'." (Yes / No / A little)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can ask for and give basic directions to a performance stage." (Yes / No / A little)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0 0 5px 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompts:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy for you in today's speaking lesson about talents and Fiestas Patrias plans?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging? How can you improve your speaking about abilities and future plans?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What aspects of the specific objective and learning outcome did students achieve successfully? (e.g., Most students could use 'can' for ability, but some struggled with the first conditional or giving clear directions.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What specific grammar points, vocabulary items, or pronunciation features need more focused practice or re-teaching in upcoming lessons? (e.g., Consistent use of 'will' vs. present simple in first conditional 'if' clauses; distinguishing rhyming words.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds might be needed for students who struggled with fluency or interactive speaking? (e.g., Provide more sentence frames or a dialogue script for lower-proficiency students; pre-teach more complex directions.)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This speaking practice has allowed students to orally rehearse ideas and sentence structures about their talents and plans. In Lesson 4, students will transfer these spoken ideas into written form, composing short paragraphs about their Fiestas Patrias performances and talents, leveraging the vocabulary and grammatical structures practiced today.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>